<section aria-labelledby="steps" class="wrap mt-16 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 id="steps" class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">The migration, step by step</h2>
  <div class="prose mt-4">
    <p>You need a server with Docker Engine 25+ and Compose 2.20+. The healthchecks use <code>start_interval</code>, which older versions don't support. On Ubuntu 24.04, install it with <a href="https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/ubuntu/">Docker's own Ubuntu instructions</a>; the distro's <code>docker.io</code> package may be older; check <code>docker version</code>.</p>

    <h3>1. Clone the repository</h3>
    <p>Cloning downloads the repository (the project's source folder) to the server.</p>
    <pre class="overflow-x-auto whitespace-pre"><code>git clone {{ site.repo }}.git openinflowwww
cd openinflowwww</code></pre>

    <h3>2. Create the network, the state files and .env</h3>
    <p>The app joins an external network called <code>n8n_default</code>, so it has to exist. (The network name comes from the maintainer's own Traefik setup; you don't need n8n.) The state is bind-mounted, so <code>proxies.json</code> has to exist as a file, not a directory; the database goes in <code>service/data/</code>.</p>
    <pre class="overflow-x-auto whitespace-pre"><code>docker network create n8n_default
echo '{"proxies": []}' &gt; service/proxies.json
mkdir -p service/data service/sessions

cat &gt; .env &lt;&lt;ENV
SHARE_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 24)
SESSION_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
CHATTER_SESSION_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
ENV</code></pre>
    <p>Without <code>SHARE_TOKEN</code> the share-link gate is off (sign-in is the only gate), and without the session secrets every rebuild signs everyone out. <code>ADMIN_PASSWORD</code> is optional. It is the only check on the admin actions (view as another user, transfer or delete a user), so anyone signed in who knows it can use them on any account; if you set it, make it long and random. Without it those actions are refused.</p>

    <h3>3. Start the stack</h3>
    <pre class="overflow-x-auto whitespace-pre"><code>docker compose up -d --build</code></pre>
    <p>
      This starts <code>openinflowwww-relay</code> (127.0.0.1:8787), <code>openinflowwww-app</code> (127.0.0.1:3001) and
      <code>openinflowwww-autoheal</code>. A server usually has no browser, so from your own computer forward the port with
      <code>ssh -L 3001:127.0.0.1:3001 user@server</code> and open <code>http://127.0.0.1:3001/?t=&lt;SHARE_TOKEN&gt;</code>
      there (<code>grep SHARE_TOKEN .env</code> on the server prints the token; it is remembered after the first visit).
      Register the owner account on the Sign in / Register screen at <code>/login</code> first. Ignore the installer
      link on the login screen; it points at an older setup.
    </p>
    <p>
      127.0.0.1 only works on the server itself, or through your <code>ssh -L</code> tunnel. Your laptop, your phone and your chatters reach the dashboard at the
      server's URL. The relay and the app bind to 127.0.0.1 on the host, and the app also joins the external
      <code>n8n_default</code> network with Traefik labels; if you run Traefik on that network it will be published.
      One way is a domain behind a reverse proxy on the server: point Caddy or nginx at <code>127.0.0.1:3001</code>, or
      use the Traefik labels in <code>docker-compose.yml</code>, which expect your own Traefik on the
      <code>n8n_default</code> network with a <code>websecure</code> entrypoint and a cert resolver named
      <code>mytlschallenge</code>. Put your host (in place of <code>your-host.example.com</code>) and those names in a
      <code>docker-compose.override.yml</code>, which the repository already ignores, so <code>git pull</code> stays
      clean; don't edit the tracked file. The other is the <code>tunnel</code> profile:
      <code>docker compose --profile tunnel up -d</code> starts a Cloudflare quick tunnel,
      <code>docker compose logs openinflowwww-tunnel</code> shows its trycloudflare.com URL, and the URL changes on every
      restart. Both make the dashboard public, so before either, read the
      <a href="{{ '/help/#security' | url }}">security notes</a>; use the quick tunnel only to try it, not for a live team.
    </p>

    <h3>4. Capture each creator's session</h3>
    {% include "capture-flow.njk" %}
    <p>
      The creator's account will be seen from the server's IP (or the proxy you assign) from then on; assign a proxy before
      pasting if that matters (step 5 shows where).
    </p>
    <p>
      The desktop app's own capture button does not work against this relay today; use the Chrome extension. The
      accounts appear in Creators &gt; Manage creators (<code>/creators</code>). If a session stops working, capture it
      again the same way.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fansly is partly built: the Docker image (a packaged copy of the app) ships the Fansly client, and 4 of the
      Fansly Manager's 16 pages are built, behind a setting.
    </p>

    <h3>5. Add your proxies</h3>
    <p>
      Assign a proxy to each creator in Creators &gt; Custom proxy (<code>/creators/proxy</code>). The list is kept in
      <code>service/proxies.json</code>, the proxy registry on the host. You supply the proxies; none are built in.
    </p>

    <h3>6. Invite your chatters</h3>
    <p>
      Invite each chatter from Employees &gt; Manage employees &gt; Chatters (<code>/employees</code>): under "Invite a new
      chatter", the "Mint invite URL" button makes a one-time <code>/chatter-login?token=...</code> link that expires after
      24 hours, and they pick a username and password there. Logins
      are a share token for the dashboard, then per-user logins: the owner at <code>/login</code>, each chatter at
      <code>/chatter-login</code>. Chatters sign in with their own login; the share token is for the owner (it does not stop anyone from registering;
      see <a href="{{ '/help/#security' | url }}">Security notes</a>). Owner pages such as Creators, Vault Pro and Tracking links are hidden from chatters.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="support" class="wrap mt-16 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 id="support" class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">Support during the move</h2>
  <div class="prose mt-4">
    <p>
      Infloww's switch page describes its private Telegram support as "Real human support, no AI"
      (infloww.com/switch-crm, page stamped Oct 10, 2026).
    </p>
    <p><strong>Ours: real human support, no AI. The human is you.</strong></p>
    <p>
      In practice that means the README, the code, and the issue tracker on <a href="{{ site.repo }}/issues">GitHub</a>.
      The LLM keys (<code>DEEPSEEK_API_KEY</code>, <code>DEEPINFRA_API_KEY</code>,
      <code>ZAI_API_KEY</code>) are optional, billed by their providers, and the dashboard works without any of them.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{#- What you keep and what you lose: two cards side by side from md up. -#}
<div class="wrap mt-16 grid max-w-3xl grid-cols-1 gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
<section aria-labelledby="keep" class="rounded-2xl bg-white p-6 ring-1 ring-black/10">
  <h2 id="keep" class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">What you keep</h2>
  <div class="prose mt-4 text-base">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Your creators and your team.</strong> Accounts come across by capturing their sessions again; chatters get new logins.</li>
      <li><strong>The core screens.</strong> Messages Pro, Smart Messages, Smart lists, Vault Pro, Scripts, Creator, Employee and Fan reports, the Message dashboard, Auto Post and Auto Story.</li>
      <li><strong>Your data, on your server.</strong> The SQLite file, the sessions and the proxy list sit next to the compose file. Back them up like passwords: <code>chatterly.db</code> and <code>proxies.json</code> hold passwords in plain text.</li>
      <li><strong>Your phone workflow, mostly.</strong> The web app is responsive, and Messages Pro shows one pane at a time on small screens.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>There is no importer from Infloww. Scripts and lists need to be set up again.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="lose" class="rounded-2xl bg-white p-6 ring-1 ring-black/10">
  <h2 id="lose" class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">What you lose</h2>
  <div class="prose mt-4 text-base">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Support and an SLA.</strong> Nobody is on call for your agency, and no uptime figure is promised.</li>
      <li><strong>Fansly, Fanvue and MYM, for now.</strong> Inflowwww supports OnlyFans. Fansly is partly built: 4 of the Fansly Manager's 16 pages.</li>
      <li><strong>Some screens.</strong> OF Manager is there, but most of its pages show placeholder data.</li>
      <li><strong>A native phone app.</strong> There isn't one.</li>
      <li><strong>Built-in proxies.</strong> You bring your own.</li>
      <li><strong>Someone else running the server.</strong> Updates, backups and restarts are your job now.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If that list decides it, read <a href="{{ '/openinflowwww-vs-infloww/#stay' | url }}">Who should stay on Infloww</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<section aria-labelledby="risk" class="wrap mt-16 max-w-3xl pb-20 sm:pb-28">
  <h2 id="risk" class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">The financial risk of your move</h2>
  <div class="prose mt-4">
    <p>
      Infloww's comparison pages say "We'll take on the financial risk of your move", with 60 days of migration
      coverage for agencies that qualify (page stamped Oct 10, 2026).
    </p>
    <p>
      We don't offer coverage, because our side of the overlap costs one server. Move one creator first, let the team settle
      in, then move the rest. Cancel the old plan only when you're sure.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

